[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-4700"] :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x({x}^{3}+1)}{3{x}^{4}-7}.$" :question/worked-solution "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{4}+2x}{3{x}^{4}-7}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{4}+2x}{3{x}^{4}-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(3-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ and $\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(2+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(3-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{2+0}{3-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{2}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-277946/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "5510eba2-a148-577b-b9dd-1062d7e1e47c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-277946/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "7d6ca3bf-89f0-5f47-90d2-b209a39b37c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-277946/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a035e5ab-32a6-5d8c-ac97-ec29000584e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-277946/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-277946/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-277946/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-277946" :question/id #uuid "9bd55036-b9b9-5eb4-a252-ea929e1a8c76" :question/math-academy-id "q-277946" :question/problem "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+3}{2-{x}^{3}}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+3}{2-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{5}{x},$ $\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}},$ and $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-1)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{4-0+0}{0-1} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-277946/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-277946"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-277950/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "1ed80573-88a0-54b0-8f21-2cf9ed1f47ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-277950/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "201eb6f5-fda9-5cd3-a7ee-5e7283a76cd9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-277950/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-277950/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-277950" :question/id #uuid "e2c3f2cc-7f24-5e07-8519-d1c4475ba3bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-277950" :question/problem "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{8{x}^{4}+6x}{({x}^{2}-2x)(1-2{x}^{2})}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{8{x}^{4}-6x}{-2{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{8{x}^{4}-6x}{-2{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{6x}{{x}^{4}})}{(-\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(8-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})}{(-2+\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}},$ $\\frac{4}{x},$ $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ and $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(8-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})}{(-2+\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{8-0}{-2+0+0-0} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-277950/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-277950"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-277944/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "e50a09ba-5999-52cc-9af4-c94ee64d2e85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-277944/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "24af47c0-2640-5c41-b998-cd09c77a329f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-277944/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c945c694-1169-5142-8b42-5f4a3989b151" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-277944/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-277944/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-277944/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-277944" :question/id #uuid "ce3539b4-30f1-5ce1-a505-47ade32dd7c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-277944" :question/problem "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-8{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}-5x}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-8{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}-5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}-8)}{(2-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{1}{x}$ and $\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}-8)}{(2-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-8}{2+0} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-277944/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-277944"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-6931/selection/f5d98a631ee48ed70accf24f8b6cebcd32363395da4e87feeaf883d85b9bc131" :answer/id #uuid "2182c1cf-1af9-50b0-bb5b-7a10057cd822" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6931/selection/3e622b14fb9e8408568d99d85f4dbc69d704ea59c7efa30ae415e06682e77843" :answer/id #uuid "09df62fa-6823-54c4-a245-bfb6fd9a4ead" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6931/selection/25eb914fe9ac4d670cc8b64d7e8deee9c4123c32ede90f0fd11dc435564dd91d" :answer/id #uuid "c1e37e1f-b27e-5cdd-84a7-4cffe2090c39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6931/selection/f7031104f14fe004994e4fe4b8af3fbe4e65cfa2bff3c7eab1748f5f2092c758" :answer/id #uuid "84e99b9a-1e5f-54d7-b601-e7075db883a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6931/selection/763a7c0b26f15df25c2db2d8ac42125e806c2e4b56e4f070b4566eba7e05dfff" :answer/id #uuid "09c9fd94-ed39-50fc-ae5c-95b8cf29e57f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6931/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "92ae7366-19e6-5142-a520-27c9d218a1d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-6931/selection/f5d98a631ee48ed70accf24f8b6cebcd32363395da4e87feeaf883d85b9bc131" :answer-field/choices ["q-6931/selection/f5d98a631ee48ed70accf24f8b6cebcd32363395da4e87feeaf883d85b9bc131" "q-6931/selection/3e622b14fb9e8408568d99d85f4dbc69d704ea59c7efa30ae415e06682e77843" "q-6931/selection/25eb914fe9ac4d670cc8b64d7e8deee9c4123c32ede90f0fd11dc435564dd91d" "q-6931/selection/f7031104f14fe004994e4fe4b8af3fbe4e65cfa2bff3c7eab1748f5f2092c758" "q-6931/selection/763a7c0b26f15df25c2db2d8ac42125e806c2e4b56e4f070b4566eba7e05dfff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-6931" :question/id #uuid "fb0a0ff9-89bd-5ff4-9372-c1be8a3ccf21" :question/math-academy-id "q-6931" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4{x}^{2}+6x+1}{1-2{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}+6x+1}{1-2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4+\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{6}{x}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(4+\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-2)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{4+0+0}{0-2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is  $y=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-6931/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-6931"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-4637/selection/96efe327aa5df1953454aa8a0acb24299d8e2e2f538b2a5f40d98e7332b886b7" :answer/id #uuid "a7a70a26-11ba-5460-b905-eae3cfb8b247" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-4637/selection/3e622b14fb9e8408568d99d85f4dbc69d704ea59c7efa30ae415e06682e77843" :answer/id #uuid "8a0163bc-cc15-5d05-ab75-079db408fe0a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-4637/selection/b4fa820c14b08b0a56aeef1698163de12603731f9057d26276152c95384fc78e" :answer/id #uuid "0b515983-8e73-5142-a419-36d643213b84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-4637/selection/72991a16d58e707659f640ae1ab762800dfb645a82c04f28c688d7ecd7444ff7" :answer/id #uuid "8a7e8a08-6276-51dc-8410-e134a6efb944" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-4637/selection/270f1fd31ad1775c84f16ab972ba8133cfe1d5cf9ffc1179a4e3a787ebba3209" :answer/id #uuid "e1bdf93f-8991-5338-97ac-b6b8bd2c1303" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-4637/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d1ce21a4-9257-5363-8037-d7ca096467f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-4637/selection/96efe327aa5df1953454aa8a0acb24299d8e2e2f538b2a5f40d98e7332b886b7" :answer-field/choices ["q-4637/selection/96efe327aa5df1953454aa8a0acb24299d8e2e2f538b2a5f40d98e7332b886b7" "q-4637/selection/3e622b14fb9e8408568d99d85f4dbc69d704ea59c7efa30ae415e06682e77843" "q-4637/selection/b4fa820c14b08b0a56aeef1698163de12603731f9057d26276152c95384fc78e" "q-4637/selection/72991a16d58e707659f640ae1ab762800dfb645a82c04f28c688d7ecd7444ff7" "q-4637/selection/270f1fd31ad1775c84f16ab972ba8133cfe1d5cf9ffc1179a4e3a787ebba3209"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-4637" :question/id #uuid "9d9688b7-9ace-5539-892f-eb62fedb06dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-4637" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x-{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{4}-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3x-{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{4}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3x}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(5-\\frac{4}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{4}{{x}^{4}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(5-\\frac{4}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-0}{5-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-4637/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-4637"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-4699/selection/f5d98a631ee48ed70accf24f8b6cebcd32363395da4e87feeaf883d85b9bc131" :answer/id #uuid "abf238ed-1aa2-5ac8-8d73-44047bc40ff0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-4699/selection/96efe327aa5df1953454aa8a0acb24299d8e2e2f538b2a5f40d98e7332b886b7" :answer/id #uuid "f9cc8590-f704-554f-b54d-c15637d1693a