g the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-3}{{x}^{3}-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{1}{x},\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0-0}{1-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-4699/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-4699"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-6998/selection/96efe327aa5df1953454aa8a0acb24299d8e2e2f538b2a5f40d98e7332b886b7" :answer/id #uuid "611aa1e6-0112-5db6-bff9-fb1ad800c6f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6998/selection/ab35b5d31934a29f264b13e06671189d17ea95205c6933037fe20d8b552aa57e" :answer/id #uuid "94cc6206-ac4d-5725-82ff-1ba17f185a68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6998/selection/bb4e1be02a1ceee34989c85bcc52e58356b4559f2810da5da714fef2710d4eca" :answer/id #uuid "e4502b38-58f9-53a0-98a8-23c423553c42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6998/selection/556c6f955c47e250ae57f4e4ae9f7e75f59b501a590beb51716e1ef6e9709b59" :answer/id #uuid "c6836e8d-4412-523c-b1ee-982f2f6e7c52" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6998/selection/3e622b14fb9e8408568d99d85f4dbc69d704ea59c7efa30ae415e06682e77843" :answer/id #uuid "c18b10d1-12dd-52c3-abca-ae54e1b47fc1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-6998/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b30c470-44d7-5081-aca5-53528b72c34d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-6998/selection/96efe327aa5df1953454aa8a0acb24299d8e2e2f538b2a5f40d98e7332b886b7" :answer-field/choices ["q-6998/selection/96efe327aa5df1953454aa8a0acb24299d8e2e2f538b2a5f40d98e7332b886b7" "q-6998/selection/ab35b5d31934a29f264b13e06671189d17ea95205c6933037fe20d8b552aa57e" "q-6998/selection/bb4e1be02a1ceee34989c85bcc52e58356b4559f2810da5da714fef2710d4eca" "q-6998/selection/556c6f955c47e250ae57f4e4ae9f7e75f59b501a590beb51716e1ef6e9709b59" "q-6998/selection/3e622b14fb9e8408568d99d85f4dbc69d704ea59c7efa30ae415e06682e77843"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-6998" :question/id #uuid "6238a11d-56f3-5197-a3d9-533c6774d65f" :question/math-academy-id "q-6998" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{8-3{x}^{3}}{1-16{x}^{4}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{8-3{x}^{3}}{1-16{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{16{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-16)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}},\\frac{3}{x}$ and  $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$  become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-16)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-0}{0-16} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-6998/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-6998"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-269988/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "c8d1141b-25e5-56ea-b417-0514bd6b08d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-269988/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "65e86732-e23d-595c-bbfc-1848f151b0df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-269988/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7cee78a3-8515-5442-ab24-23c06e0410b1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-269988/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-269988/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-269988/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-269988" :question/id #uuid "beaf7cea-b83a-5727-986d-b09c501e0dda" :question/math-academy-id "q-269988" :question/problem "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{3}+2x-5}{{x}^{2}(2{x}^{2}+3)}$ is $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The given function can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{3}+2x-5}{2{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{7{x}^{3}+2x-5}{2{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}})}{(2+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{7}{x},$ $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},$ $\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}},$ and $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}})}{(2+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0-0}{2+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-269988/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-269988"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-7006"] :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{9x+8{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{9x+8{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{x}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}},\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{8}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{x}-1)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{0+0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-81202/selection/3e622b14fb9e8408568d99d85f4dbc69d704ea59c7efa30ae415e06682e77843" :answer/id #uuid "f25d8501-57eb-53cb-b741-a728d66ca2bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81202/selection/2e12ba8925bf524503f2444b8e9ba58bc68b822391e7a93f8ae4a9f7c858f0bb" :answer/id #uuid "87a99ee2-6993-54a2-815b-2add5019df67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81202/selection/25eb914fe9ac4d670cc8b64d7e8deee9c4123c32ede90f0fd11dc435564dd91d" :answer/id #uuid "b50ddb3f-b8b8-50ae-aaae-a9cf0c338a82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81202/selection/bb4e1be02a1ceee34989c85bcc52e58356b4559f2810da5da714fef2710d4eca" :answer/id #uuid "d3a2c4c6-6ade-5677-bab5-393c685a78e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81202/selection/f5d98a631ee48ed70accf24f8b6cebcd32363395da4e87feeaf883d85b9bc131" :answer/id #uuid "cbc30b36-6a79-5afc-bab5-ee81e205e754" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81202/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e329da5d-bd84-5401-8353-d6606dbeb32d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-81202/selection/3e622b14fb9e8408568d99d85f4dbc69d704ea59c7efa30ae415e06682e77843" :answer-field/choices ["q-81202/selection/3e622b14fb9e8408568d99d85f4dbc69d704ea59c7efa30ae415e06682e77843" "q-81202/selection/2e12ba8925bf524503f2444b8e9ba58bc68b822391e7a93f8ae4a9f7c858f0bb" "q-81202/selection/25eb914fe9ac4d670cc8b64d7e8deee9c4123c32ede90f0fd11dc435564dd91d" "q-81202/selection/bb4e1be02a1ceee34989c85bcc52e58356b4559f2810da5da714fef2710d4eca" "q-81202/selection/f5d98a631ee48ed70accf24f8b6cebcd32363395da4e87feeaf883d85b9bc131"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81202" :question/id #uuid "225d5964-b5f6-5f9c-9606-7856be911138" :question/math-academy-id "q-81202" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{3+4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3+4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+4-2x)}{(1-\\frac{1}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{1}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+4-2x)}{(1-\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0+4-2x}{1-0} \\\\  & =4-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-81202/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81202"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-7005/selection/bb008056b734e47f94938979144de7764e03b5c7b6f9fc07ff77ecb735c20148" :answer/id #uuid "3fffd3ff-b886-5a06-b9ba-d88401ed5c73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-7005/selection/96efe327aa5df1953454aa8a0acb24299d8e2e2f538b2a5f40d98e7332b886b7" :answer/id #uuid "134c2ddc-30e7-52ff-b31e-4810ecaa1efd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-7005/selection/feb2c353acce484cb267fc95bae1ab6db185f770de87fe9eb4e168b5aa1d3a8e" :answer/id #uuid "1022a364-3776-584c-94d9-993561f7365d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-7005/selection/c3deb073f152a478e46392e1f56479f2987a022223419d7f9c369a815039d205" :answer/id #uuid "45857523-5a83-508b-98cc-ffde7368f930" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-7005/selection/3e622b14fb9e8408568d99d85f4dbc69d704ea59c7efa30ae415e06682e77843" :answer/id #uuid "e9fb6b11-5a63-5e40-8e45-2e050a63e959" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-7005/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3c6a6827-37f6-5cf6-a794-7a448c22e51e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-7005/selection/3e622b14fb9e8408568d99d85f4dbc69d704ea59c7efa30ae415e06682e77843" :answer-field/choices ["q-7005/selection/bb008056b734e47f94938979144de7764e03b5c7b6f9fc07ff77ecb735c20148" "q-7005/selection/96efe327aa5df1953454aa8a0acb24299d8e2e2f538b2a5f40d98e7332b886b7" "q-7005/selection/feb2c353acce484cb267fc95bae1ab6db185f770de87fe9eb4e168b5aa1d3a8e" "q-7005/selection/c3deb073f152a478e46392e1f56479f2987a022223419d7f9c369a815039d205" "q-7005/selection/3e622b14fb9e8408568d99d85f4dbc69d704ea59c7efa30ae415e06682e77843"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-7005" :question/id #uuid "fbaacded-fa5e-53e5-bb47-913c990f1827" :question/math-academy-id "q-7005" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{6-3x+4{x}^{2}}{12x-21}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{6-3x+4{x}^{2}}{12x-21} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{x})}{(\\frac{12x}{x}-\\frac{21}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{6}{x}-3+4x)}{(12-\\frac{21}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{6}{x}$ and $\\frac{21}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{6}{x}-3+4x)}{(12-\\frac{21}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0-3+4x}{12-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x-3}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-7005/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-7005"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-6993"] :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}+3}{4{x}^{2}-6x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+3}{4{x}^{2}-6x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(4-\\frac{6}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{6}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(4-\\frac{6}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{x+0}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-113178/selection/3e622b14fb9e8408568d99d85f4dbc69d704ea59c7efa30ae415e06682e77843" :answer/id #uuid "2e6d2b9b-4fc5-55f5-b0b8-4d24a4f93df6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113178/selection/dbaf85835946aefb97564ba0d1ea287b684a4c8821f4256502e349376df74d28" :answer/id #uuid "98ec2de6-efe2-5639-97e3-70eed9db85f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113178/selection/96efe327aa5df1953454aa8a0acb24299d8e2e2f538b2a5f40d98e7332b886b7" :answer/id #uuid "1bfb1810-7ea8-57b7-9b69-530c586fb05f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113178/selection/f7031104f14fe004994e4fe4b8af3fbe4e65cfa2bff3c7eab1748f5f2092c758" :answer/id #uuid "cb1d8512-c4a3-5f5f-a68b-5671b307a8ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113178/selection/763a7c0b26f15df25c2db2d8ac42125e806c2e4b56e4f070b4566eba7e05dfff" :answer/id #uuid "d58c7b64-8918-5e5e-aec6-48bf6fa9c2b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113178/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "026eb333-7fe3-509a-a7be-815358835cf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-113178/selection/3e622b14fb9e8408568d99d85f4dbc69d704ea59c7efa30ae415e06682e77843" :answer-field/choices ["q-113178/selection/3e622b14fb9e8408568d99d85f4dbc69d704ea59c7efa30ae415e06682e77843" "q-113178/selection/dbaf85835946aefb97564ba0d1ea287b684a4c8821f4256502e349376df74d28" "q-113178/selection/96efe327aa5df1953454aa8a0acb24299d8e2e2f538b2a5f40d98e7332b886b7" "q-113178/selection/f7031104f14fe004994e4fe4b8af3fbe4e65cfa2bff3c7eab1748f5f2092c758" "q-113178/selection/763a7c0b26f15df25c2db2d8ac42125e806c2e4b56e4f070b4566eba7e05dfff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-113178" :question/id #uuid "98127c5e-809c-59c2-bd79-ffd78a0e8150" :question/math-academy-id "q-113178" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}-3x+2}{{x}^{2}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{4}-3x+2}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{x},$ $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{{x}^{2}-0+0}{1+0} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-113178/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-113178"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-4003/selection/3e622b14fb9e8408568d99d85f4dbc69d704ea59c7efa30ae415e06682e77843" :answer/id #uuid "68a1f178-1a55-5d78-945a-f72ae95a88d6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-4003/selection/4a66193ecde95bd0a4392b50d15d70e0aa0b182a541f9b9826b6986a1c19387a" :answer/id #uuid "a94108af-99f3-5baf-96c1-18259cd30300" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-4003/selection/96efe327aa5df1953454aa8a0acb24299d8e2e2f538b2a5f40d98e7332b886b7" :answer/id #uuid "5a95b8fe-da81-5d82-838f-4bf246fa322e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-4003/selection/2e12ba8925bf524503f2444b8e9ba58bc68b822391e7a93f8ae4a9f7c858f0bb" :answer/id #uuid "1f7bdf8b-0782-5c1f-ab53-4f6ca30d5393" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-4003/selection/763a7c0b26f15df25c2db2d8ac42125e806c2e4b56e4f070b4566eba7e05dfff" :answer/id #uuid "5ec79457-133e-5aa0-bdb1-0a4606a5fe44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-4003/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1d6b794f-a08f-5ebb-a767-5d926c975bec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-4003/selection/3e622b14fb9e8408568d99d85f4dbc69d704ea59c7efa30ae415e06682e77843" :answer-field/choices ["q-4003/selection/3e622b14fb9e8408568d99d85f4dbc69d704ea59c7efa30ae415e06682e77843" "q-4003/selection/4a66193ecde95bd0a4392b50d15d70e0aa0b182a541f9b9826b6986a1c19387a" "q-4003/selection/96efe327aa5df1953454aa8a0acb24299d8e2e2f538b2a5f40d98e7332b886b7" "q-4003/selection/2e12ba8925bf524503f2444b8e9ba58bc68b822391e7a93f8ae4a9f7c858f0bb" "q-4003/selection/763a7c0b26f15df25c2db2d8ac42125e806c2e4b56e4f070b4566eba7e05dfff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-4003" :question/id #uuid "6e310bd5-107e-5272-9b9a-310ea9e9c58c" :question/math-academy-id "q-4003" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}+2x+1}{{x}^{2}-x-12}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+2x+1}{{x}^{2}-x-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{x},\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{x}$ and $\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{x+0+0}{1-0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{1} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-4003/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-4003"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5847"] :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{4x^3-x}{3x^3+24}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^3.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}-x}{3{x}^{3}+24} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ and $\\dfrac{24}{x^3}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{4-0}{3+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = \\dfrac{4}{3}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5849"] :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^4.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x}{6{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms  $\\dfrac{3}{x^2}, \\dfrac{2}{x^3},$ and $\\dfrac{1}{x^4}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = 0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5851"] :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x) = \\dfrac{2x^3}{x^2-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^2.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{2x}{1-0} \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y = f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
